can help identify performance bottlenecks or errors in specific services. \n\nBut first, maybe it's better to check the current state of each service. The `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with `kubectl get pods` and `kubectl get deployments` could provide information on the status of pods and deployments. If any pods are crashing or deployments are not stable, that's a sign of a problem. \n\nHowever, the user might not have access to kubectl commands, or maybe the tools are limited. Wait, the available tools include `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd`, so using that to get pod and deployment statuses makes sense. For example, checking if all pods are running and deployments are healthy. \n\nAlternatively, using `get_metrics` with specific PromQL queries could show if any services are consuming abnormal resources or have high error rates. But without knowing the exact metrics, it's a bit vague. \n\nAnother angle: the `get_dependency_graph` from Jaeger could show if there are any unusual dependencies or latency issues between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might indicate a fault. \n\nBut since the user wants to go deep into the root cause, starting with checking the current state of the services (pods and deployments) would be foundational. If there are any immediately obvious issues there, that's a good starting point. If not, then moving to metrics and traces. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to use `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` to get the list of pods and deployments in the namespace. That would help identify if any services have pods that are not running or deployments that are not in a healthy state. \n\nTherefore, the tool choice should be `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with the command `kubectl get pods` and `kubectl get deployments` to check the current state of the workloads.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo assess the current state of the application, I need to check the status of pods and deployments in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. This will reveal if any services are not running, have crashes, or deployment issues. Using `kubectl get pods` and `kubectl get deployments` will provide critical health and readiness information for the microservices.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 649, 'prompt_tokens': 1679, 'total_tokens': 2328, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-93e0-7d92-af23-ee82d151bcca-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1679, 'output_tokens': 649, 'total_tokens': 2328}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c5ca6b1c-a681-48c1-95ab-b56bfbbd00dd'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call for the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The previous step was to get the list of services using get_services, which returned frontend-proxy, load-generator, image-provider, ad, and frontend. Now, the next step is to check the status of the pods and deployments in the astronomy-shop namespace.\n\nI need to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. The available commands include 'kubectl get pods' and 'kubectl get deployments'. Since the user mentioned checking pods and deployments, I should run both commands. However, the tool allows only one command per call. So, I should split them into two separate tool calls. But the user is asking for the next tool call, so maybe start with getting the pods first. \n\nWait, the user's last message was to choose a tool and justify. They chose exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, so the next step is to generate the tool call. The command should be 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop' to check the pod statuses. Then, after that, another call for deployments. But in this step, I need to make one tool call. So, first, get the pods. That makes sense because pods are the smallest units, and checking their status would show if any are not running or have errors. Then, deployments can be checked next to see if they are healthy and have the correct replicas.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8079f2e9d642d2ed', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 321, 'prompt_tokens': 2660, 'total_tokens': 2981, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-b151-7ed2-9b1c-3fd7bdff4b5a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8079f2e9d642d2ed', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2660, 'output_tokens': 321, 'total_tokens': 2981}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-2lvtc        1/1     Running   0          5m51s\nad-6f9964ffd8-xw5kj                1/1     Running   0          5m51s\ncart-65474cd577-vkfwt              1/1     Running   0          5m51s\ncheckout-678cf8fcd7-fkhs7          0/1     Pending   0          35s\ncheckout-6bc76865c-w5kft           0/1     Pending   0          29s\ncurrency-69f5b55bbc-xcmcv          1/1     Running   0          5m50s\nemail-56985bb4f8-zltss             1/1     Running   0          5m49s\nflagd-79c4c6cb44-z4wqg             2/2     Running   0          5m48s\nfraud-detection-88b76487c-54dv6    1/1     Running   0          5m49s\nfrontend-65c7cd5577-hf