</tool_call> - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e4ab0a1c-c29a-43d6-bc00-cc9f0ba67f9d'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6e7a85d4-f9a1-434b-aaa9-dbcd9131a41a'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e1d07dd0-9f20-4b46-87e4-b62504c7b40f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any failed transactions or long-running requests. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might be a root cause. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each one.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 411, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1903, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-6321-72c2-9107-3b421d3b470c-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 411, 'total_tokens': 1903}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c067e3ff-a1cd-40d2-9db9-fbf7af5518da'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster, specifically in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The user mentioned using the get_services tool, which retrieves service names from Grafana. \n\nWait, but the tool description says it gets the list from Grafana. However, the application in question is the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is in the astronomy-shop namespace. Should I specify the namespace when using get_services? The tool's parameters don't mention a namespace argument. Hmm, maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to that namespace, or perhaps the tool doesn't require it. The user's initial instructions say to check the namespace, so maybe I need to confirm that.\n\nBut the get_services tool's parameters are empty, so it doesn't take any arguments. That means it might return all services in the cluster, not just the astronomy-shop namespace. That could be a problem. Wait, maybe the Grafana instance the tool is querying is already filtered to the correct namespace. Alternatively, maybe I need to use kubectl to list the services in the namespace first. But the user hasn't mentioned using kubectl yet. \n\nWait, the user's initial setup says the application belongs to the astronomy-shop namespace. So perhaps the get_services tool, when called, will return services specific to that namespace. But since the tool's parameters don't include a namespace, maybe it's designed to work within that context. Alternatively, maybe I need to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the services in the namespace. For example, running 'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop'. \n\nBut the user's first step was to use get_services. Maybe the Grafana instance is already set up to show services in that namespace. However, if the tool doesn't take a namespace parameter, it might not be accurate. But since the user instructed to start with get_services, I should proceed with that. \n\nSo the tool call would be to get_services with no arguments. The response would give a list of services. Then, based on that list, I can proceed to check each service's health, metrics, dependencies, etc. \n\nAlternatively, if the get_services tool isn't scoped to the namespace, the list might include services from other namespaces, which would complicate things. But given the user's setup, perhaps the Grafana instance is already configured for the astronomy-shop namespace. \n\nIn any case, the first step is to get the list of services. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. Then, once I have the services, I can move on to checking their metrics, traces, and dependencies.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-98aeabe73c954116', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 579, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3052, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-76cc-79b2-a753-a1d67c05bbba-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-98aeabe73c954116', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 579, 'total_tokens': 3052}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'frontend-proxy\', \'load-generator\', \'image-provider\', \'ad\', \'frontend\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='1b01e3fb-6e27-4ce5-9fd1-7a27587b385e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-98aeabe73c954116'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b4ff8619-5b3a-4fc5-8ccd-7e8c1239b31f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to monitor and diagnose the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and their root causes. Let me recap what's been done so far.\n\nFirst, the assistant used the `get_services` tool to list all services in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The response included services like frontend-proxy, load-generator, image-provider, ad, and frontend. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty. \n\nSince the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, the approach should be systematic. The assistant needs to check each service's health, metrics, dependencies, and traces. But how to proceed?\n\nLooking at the available tools, `get_dependency_graph` could show how services interact, which might reveal issues like broken dependencies or unexpected traffic patterns. Alternatively, `get_traces` can help identify performance bottlenecks or errors in specific services. \n\nBut first, maybe it's better to check the current state of each service. The `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with `kubectl get pods` and `kubectl get deployments` could provide information on the status of pods and deployments. If any pods are crashing or deployments are not stable, that's a sign of a problem. \n\nHowever, the user might not have access to kubectl commands, or maybe the tools are limited. Wait, the available tools include `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd`, so using that to get pod and deployment statuses makes sense. For example, checking if all pods are running and deployments are healthy. \n\nAlternatively, using `get_metrics` with specific PromQL queries could show if any services are consuming abnormal resources or have high error rates. But without knowing the exact metrics, it's a bit vague. \n\nAnother angle: the `get_dependency_graph` from Jaeger could show if there are any unusual dependencies or latency issues between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might indicate a fault. \n\nBut since the user wants to go deep into the root cause, starting with checking the current state of the services (pods and deployments) would be foundational. If there are any immediately obvious issues there, that's a good starting point. If not, then moving to metrics and traces. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to use `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` to get the list of pods and deployments in the namespace. That would help identify if any services have pods that are not running or deployments that are not in a healthy state. \n\nTherefore, the tool choice should be `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with the command `kubectl get pods` and `kubectl get deployments` to check the current state of the workloads.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo assess the current state of the application, I need to check the status of pods and deployments in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. This will reveal if any services are not running, have crashes, or deployment issues. Using `kubectl get pods` and `kubectl get deployments` will provide critical health and readiness information for the microservices.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 649, 'prompt_tokens': 1679, 'total_tokens': 2328, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-93e0-7d92-af23-ee82d151bcca-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1679, 'output_tokens': 649, 'total_tokens': 2328}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c5ca6b1c-a681-48c1-95ab-b56bfbbd00dd'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call for the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The previous step was to get the list of services using get_services, which returned frontend-proxy, load-generator, image-provider, ad, and frontend. Now, the next step is to check the status of the pods and deployments in the astronomy-shop namespace.\n\nI need to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. The available commands include 'kubectl get pods' and 'kubectl get deployments'. Since the user mentioned checking pods and deployments, I should run both commands. However, the tool allows only one command per call. So, I should split them into two separate tool calls. But the user is asking for the next tool call, so maybe start with getting the pods first. \n\nWait, the user's last message was to choose a tool and justify. They chose exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, so the next step is to generate the tool call. The command should be 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop' to check the pod statuses. Then, after that, another call for deployments. But in this step, I need to make one tool call. So, first, get the pods. That makes sense because pods are the smallest units, and checking their status would show if any are not running or have errors. Then, deployments can be checked next to see if they are healthy and have the correct replicas.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8079f2e9d642d2ed', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 321, 'prompt_tokens': 2660, 'total_tokens': 2981, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-b151-7ed2-9b1c-3fd7bdff4b5a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8079f2e9d642d2ed', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2660, 'output_tokens': 321, 'total_tokens': 2981}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-2lvtc        1/1     Running   0          5m51s\nad-6f9964ffd8-xw5kj                1/1     Running   0          5m51s\ncart-65474cd577-vkfwt              1/1     Running   0          5m51s\ncheckout-678cf8fcd7-fkhs7          0/1     Pending   0          35s\ncheckout-6bc76865c-w5kft           0/1     Pending   0          29s\ncurrency-69f5b55bbc-xcmcv          1/1     Running   0          5m50s\nemail-56985bb4f8-zltss             1/1     Running   0          5m49s\nflagd-79c4c6cb44-z4wqg             2/2     Running   0          5m48s\nfraud-detection-88b76487c-54dv6    1/1     Running   0          5m49s\nfrontend-65c7cd5577-hfdrh          1/1     Running   0          5m51s\nfrontend-proxy-84788b44b-h2slt     1/1     Running   0          5m51s\ngrafana-bdbfb995f-lzg4p            4/4     Running   0          5m48s\nimage-provider-6c7d4665cc-b88jk    1/1     Running   0          5m50s\njaeger-6d6f47c768-mrn8v            1/1     Running   0          5m51s\nkafka-74ccb7b5fd-56grv             1/1     Running   0          5m50s\nllm-55b9d48bb4-hkpgl               1/1     Running   0          5m50s\nload-generator-9895cc8bf-kdq26     1/1     Running   0          5m48s\nlocust-fetcher                     1/1     Running   0          46s\nopensearch-0                       1/1     Running   0          5m51s\notel-collector-agent-cpkhh         1/1     Running   0          35s\notel-collector-agent-n55zj         1/1     Running   0          28s\notel-collector-agent-sgf9v         1/1     Running   0          43s\npayment-fbfc99665-8x5sr            1/1     Running   0          5m50s\npostgresql-68c5b8f59b-k9fmj        1/1     Running   0          5m51s\nproduct-catalog-f57789b6d-zlkpg    1/1     Running   0          5m50s\nproduct-reviews-6f447976bc-479wg   1/1     Running   0          5m50s\nquote-bc9b88d7d-tjjcx              1/1     Running   0          5m51s\nrecommendation-77b6cd7dc6-9t55j    1/1     Running   0          5m48s\nshipping-54f9758cb5-ff9p9          1/1     Running   0          5m50s\nvalkey-cart-6c4f7cf8f6-kb67f       1/1     Running   0          5m51s\n', id='48128f0f-8d89-40bb-8a8a-b19510c26ced', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8079f2e9d642d2ed'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='728e117a-6f8e-448b-ae65-cb79a6b6f076'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they\'ve used get_services to list the services and exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the pods. The pod output shows that some services like checkout have pods in Pending status. That\'s a red flag. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the checkout pods are pending. The next step is to check the deployments for the checkout service. If the pods are pending, it might be due to issues like resource limits, node availability, or deployment configuration problems. \n\nThe user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, so they can run kubectl get deployments for the checkout service. That would show the deployment status, replicas, and any errors. If the deployment is not scaling properly or there are rollout issues, that could explain the pending pods.\n\nAlternatively, checking the events with kubectl describe pods might give more details on why the pods can\'t start. But since the user is focusing on deployments, starting with the deployment status makes sense. \n\nAnother angle is checking the service accounts and permissions, but that\'s more advanced. The immediate step is to look at the deployment configuration. So, using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop" would provide the necessary info. If the deployment is not creating pods, there might be issues with the replica set or image pull problems. \n\nAlso, the user might need to check the logs of the pending pods, but since the tool is read-only, they can\'t do that directly. However, the deployment status can indicate if the pods are stuck in pending due to resource constraints or other deployment issues. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check the deployments for the checkout service to see if there are any issues with the deployment itself.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe `checkout` service has pods in a `Pending` state, which indicates potential issues with deployment, resource allocation, or dependencies. To investigate, I will check the deployment status of the `checkout` service using `kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop` to identify if there are rollout errors, replica set failures, or configuration issues causing the pods to remain in a pending state.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 465, 'prompt_tokens': 2801, 'total_tokens': 3266, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-c416-7ba0-9015-a3b273480c91-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2801, 'output_tokens': 465, 'total_tokens': 3266}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8d6445e9-1f10-4d69-bf31-3304232163e1'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve already used the get_services tool to list the services and then checked the pods using kubectl get pods. They noticed that the checkout service has pods in Pending state. Now, they need to find out why those pods aren\'t running.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the deployments for the checkout service. The user mentioned using kubectl get deployments to see if there are rollout errors or replica set issues. So, the tool to use here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd again, but this time with the command to get deployments in the astronomy-shop namespace. \n\nWait, the command should be "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop" to specifically check the deployments. The user might need to see the deployment details, like the number of replicas, available replicas, and any errors in the rollout. If the deployment is not progressing, it could be due to resource constraints, image pull issues, or misconfigurations. \n\nBy running kubectl get deployments, they can check the status of the checkout deployment. If there are errors, the output might show something like 0/2 replicas available, or a rollout stuck in pending. This information will help identify the root cause, such as insufficient resources, incorrect environment variables, or problems with the service account permissions. \n\nSo, the correct tool call is to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop". This will provide the necessary details about the checkout deployment\'s status and any potential issues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8545a6e1535b31c5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 357, 'prompt_tokens': 3790, 'total_tokens': 4147, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfafc-da48-74e2-9b73-2ef5ecfa7889-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8545a6e1535b31c5', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3790, 'output_tokens': 357, 'total_tokens': 4147}), ToolMessage(content='NAME              READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\naccounting        1/1     1            1           6m1s\nad                1/1     1            1           6m1s\ncart              1/1     1            1           6m1s\ncheckout          0/1     1            0           45s\ncurrency          1/1     1            1           6m1s\nemail             1/1     1            1           6m1s\nflagd             1/1     1            1           6m1s\nfraud-detection   1/1     1            1           6m1s\nfrontend          1/1     1            1           6m1s\nfrontend-proxy    1/1     1            1           6m1s\ngrafana           1/1     1            1           6m1s\nimage-provider    1/1     1            1           6m1s\njaeger            1/1     1            1           6m1s\nkafka             1/1     1            1           6m1s\nllm               1/1     1            1           6m1s\nload-generator    1/1     1            1           6m1s\npayment           1/1     1            1           6m1s\npostgresql        1/1     1            1           6m1s\nproduct-catalog   1/1     1            1           6m1s\nproduct-reviews   1/1     1            1           6m1s\nquote             1/1     1            1           6m1s\nrecommendation    1/1     1            1           6m1s\nshipping          1/1     1            1           6m1s\nvalkey-cart       1/1     1            1           6m1s\n', id='98d565d7-fc37-4732-83a9-238de746567e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8545a6e1535b31c5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4fdfa4bd-47e1-43db-ade1-d32d5e3d3955')]]
